### 2.2 [run_farmer_case.py](file:///c:/Users/pc/Desktop/GitWorkspace2026/219_simplex_fv/pid_simplex_fv/run_farmer_case.py) — One-line change
[Line 212](file:///c:/Users/pc/Desktop/GitWorkspace2026/219_simplex_fv/pid_simplex_fv/run_farmer_case.py#L212)

```diff
+        split_mode=2,  # Mode 2: kink-plane splitting
```

To revert to Mode 1, change `split_mode=2` to `split_mode=1`.

## 3. Execution Flow Comparison

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Start iteration<br/>(evaluate all simplices)"] --> B["Identify LB simplex"]
    B --> C{"split_mode?"}
    
    C -->|"Mode 1"| D["Select candidate point<br/>(ms/c_s from LB simplex)"]
    D --> E["Insert single point<br/>(subdivide one simplex)"]
    E --> F["Evaluate Q at new node"]
    F --> G["Re-evaluate mesh → end of iter"]
    
    C -->|"Mode 2"| H["Extract ms points from LB simplex"]
    H --> I["Detect kink plane<br/>(ms coord ∉ vertex coords)"]
    I --> J{"Kink found?"}
    J -->|"Yes"| K["Cut ALL straddling simplices<br/>(split_by_hyperplane)"]
    K --> L["Evaluate Q at all new nodes"]
    L --> M["Re-evaluate mesh"]
    M --> N{"Converged?"}
    N -->|"No"| A
    N -->|"Yes"| O["Done"]
    J -->|"No"| D
```

**Key difference:** Mode 2's inner loop (A→H→I→K→L→M→N→A) operates at the **whole-mesh level**, cutting potentially dozens of simplices per pass, while Mode 1's loop splits exactly one simplex. Mode 2 only falls back to Mode 1 when no new kink planes remain to cut.

## 4. Test Results (Farmer Smoke Test)

| Cut # | Time | Kink Plane | Nodes | LB / scenario | UB / scenario | Rel. Gap |
|:------:|------:|:-----------|------:|--------------:|--------------:|---------:|
| 1 | 0.8s | x₃(beets) = 300 | 7 | −114,078 | −98,200 | 16.17% |
| 2 | 3.5s | x₁(wheat) = 167 | 14 | −114,078 | −107,100 | 6.52% |
| 3 | 7.5s | x₂(corn) = 67 | 23 | −113,711 | −108,367 | 4.93% |
| 4 | 28.6s | x₂(corn) = 150 | 54 | −113,711 | −108,367 | 4.93% |
| 5 | 59.6s | x₁(wheat) = 83 | 103 | −112,600 | −108,367 | 3.91% |
| 6 | 116.0s | x₁(wheat) | 177 | **−109,999** | **−108,367** | **1.51%** |

**Terminated at 177 nodes** (target was 120). Final gap: **1.51%** with UB within 0.02% of optimal (−108,390).

> [!NOTE]
> For comparison, Mode 1 achieved only ~5.3% gap after 116 single-point iterations on the same problem.

## 5. Observations and Design Trade-offs

### Why all cuts happen in "iteration 0"
Mode 2 uses `continue` to restart the loop after each hyperplane cut. This means the `for it` counter never increments — all 6 cuts appear as sub-iterations of iteration 0. This is intentional: each cut exposes new kink planes (because the re-evaluated ms points now lie in refined simplices), creating a cascade of refinements that should be performed immediately rather than waiting for the next outer iteration.

### Simplex count growth
The number of simplices grows faster than Mode 1 because each hyperplane cut slices **many** simplices simultaneously:

| After cut | Simplices | Growth factor |
|-----------|-----------|--------------|
| Initial | 1 | — |
| Cut 1 | 4 | 4× |
| Cut 2 | 18 | 4.5× |
| Cut 3 | 39 | 2.2× |
| Cut 4 | 175 | 4.5× |
| Cut 5 | 381 | 2.2× |
| Cut 6 | 658 | 1.7× |

The growth factor diminishes as more simplices are already aligned with previously-cut planes (and are thus not straddling).

### UB convergence via EF
The UB converges rapidly because the EF (Extensive Form) solver finds near-optimal first-stage decisions even on the initial mesh. The EF upper bound was within 0.02% of the known optimal from the very first cut. The LB side is what primarily drives the remaining gap.

### Fallback to Mode 1
When Mode 2 exhausts all detectable kink planes (ms points coincide with vertex coordinates), it silently falls through to Mode 1 for that iteration. This ensures the algorithm doesn't stall — it just switches to the original fine-grained refinement strategy.

## 6. Potential Improvements

1. **Plane deduplication across iterations**: Currently, Mode 2 may re-detect a plane that was already cut if the ms point happens to land on the same coordinate again. Adding a "cut planes history" set would prevent redundant (no-op) cuts.

2. **Budget-aware cutting**: When the mesh is already large, cutting along a new plane may create hundreds of new simplices. A threshold could limit Mode 2 to only cut when the expected number of new simplices is within a budget.

3. **Non-axis-aligned planes**: The current implementation only handles axis-aligned hyperplanes (`x[axis] = value`). For problems with non-separable recourse, kink planes may be oblique — supporting general hyperplanes would require a more complex intersection algorithm.
